<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azores</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Azores are an archipelago of nine volcanic islands in the North Atlantic, about 1,400 kilometres west of mainland Portugal. Settled by the Portuguese from the 1430s, they served as a waystation for ships returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia. In the civil war of the 1830s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terceira">Terceira</a> was a stronghold of the liberals against Miguel, and the islands are now an autonomous region of Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azores.jpg" alt="Azores" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tonyjeff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Azores.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Azores lie in the North Atlantic in three groups spread over more than 600 km: Flores and Corvo to the west; Graciosa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terceira">Terceira</a>, São Jorge, Pico and Faial in the centre; and São Miguel, Santa Maria and the Formigas islets to the east. Together the islands cover 2,346 km2, from São Miguel, the largest at 759 km2, to Corvo, the smallest at about 17 km2. Mount Pico, at 2,351 m, is the highest point in Portugal. The archipelago sits over a triple junction where three tectonic plates meet. The climate is mild, wet and often cloudy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The discovery is poorly documented. A captain sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, possibly Gonçalo Velho, may have found the islands in 1427, but this is not certain. From 1433 Gonçalo Velho Cabral gathered settlers, and in 1436 he sailed to establish colonies, first on Santa Maria. Many settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a>. By 1490 some 2,000 Flemings lived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terceira">Terceira</a>, Pico, Faial, São Jorge and Flores, so that the islands became known as the Flemish Islands.</p><p>In 1522 an earthquake and landslide devastated Vila Franca do Campo, then the capital of São Miguel, and killed about 5,000 people; the capital moved to Ponta Delgada.  After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-king-of-portugal">Cardinal-King Henry</a> in 1580, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> claimed the Portuguese crown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> held the Azores under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberian-union">Iberian Union</a> from 1580 to 1642. The islands were the last part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> to resist him, until the Conquest of the Azores in 1583. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-wars">Portuguese Civil War</a> of 1828–1834 the liberals won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia</a> da Vitória in 1829, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terceira">Terceira</a> became the headquarters of the new regime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Azores are an autonomous region of Portugal, and Ponta Delgada is the largest city. The main industries are agriculture, dairy farming, livestock, fishing and tourism. The culture, dialect, cuisine and traditions vary considerably from island to island, because the remote islands were settled sporadically over about two centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azores” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator its sailors settled Madeira and the <b>Azores</b>, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese seafarers then settled Madeira and the <b>Azores</b>, traded and raided along the West African coast, and began sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The bull Inter caetera drew a line west of the <b>Azores</b>, granting Spain lands beyond it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Portugal stayed neutral in the Second World War, leased the <b>Azores</b> to the Allies and joined NATO in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo (Portugal)</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese forces defeated his army at Coolela in 1895 and captured him at Chaimite; he died in exile in the <b>Azores</b>, and the land became part of Portuguese Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Miguel abolished the Charter and ruled as an absolute monarch, persecuting liberals, many of whom fled abroad or to the <b>Azores</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/miguelist-portugal">Miguelist Portugal</a></li></ul></section>
